# The reference frame corpus is 599 MB of read-only evidence. It must never enter
# a build context, a container image or a client bundle.
screenshots/

# The specification catalog and vendor documentation are inputs, not artifacts.
docs/
blitzy/
mkdocs.yml

# Version-control metadata and local tooling state.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/

# Dependencies and build output are installed or produced inside the image.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
.turbo/
**/.turbo/
.vite/
**/.vite/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and coverage artifacts.
coverage/
**/coverage/
playwright-report/
**/playwright-report/
test-results/
**/test-results/

# Local secrets and scratch files never travel into an image. A populated environment
# file carries the session secret and the object-storage credentials, and anything
# baked into an image layer is readable by anyone who can pull that image, so every
# level of the tree is covered rather than the root alone. The committed template is
# the single exception, re-admitted at any depth: it holds documented, non-sensitive
# defaults and no credential. Logs are covered for the same reason: the server emits
# structured audit records, so a stray log file is a disclosure risk, not just noise.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example
*.log
**/*.log
.venv/
venv/
site/

# Editor and operating-system noise. None of it is a build input, and admitting it
# would let the image contents vary with whoever happened to run the build.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
**/Thumbs.db
.idea/
**/.idea/
.vscode/
**/.vscode/
